Package: polychoice
Type: Package
Title: Multinomial Choice, Inflated Multinomial Logit, and Tests of IIA
Version: 0.1.0
Authors@R: person("Benjamin E.", "Bagozzi", email = "bagozzib@udel.edu",
    role = c("aut", "cre"), comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0002-6233-6453"))
Description: Estimation, inference, quantities of interest, and specification
    tests for unordered polytomous (multinomial) choice outcomes. Fits the
    multinomial and conditional logit with case-specific and alternative-specific
    covariates, varying choice sets, frequency weights, offsets, unit fixed
    effects (with a split-panel jackknife bias correction), and analytic,
    outer-product, robust, cluster-robust, or bootstrap standard errors; the
    baseline-inflated multinomial logit of Bagozzi and Marchetti (2017)
    <doi:10.1017/psrm.2015.42> for any inflated alternative, and with several
    inflated alternatives the parametrized logit captivity model of Swait and
    Ben-Akiva (1987) <doi:10.1016/0191-2615(87)90010-5>, which generalizes the
    dogit of Gaudry and Dagenais (1979) <doi:10.1016/0191-2615(79)90028-6>; the
    two-level nested
    logit, the heteroskedastic extreme value model, the multinomial probit, and
    the multinomial logit with random alternative-specific intercepts; the
    rank-ordered logit (Beggs, Cardell and Hausman 1981
    <doi:10.1016/0304-4076(81)90056-7>) for complete, partial, and tied
    rankings (Allison and Christakis 1994 <doi:10.2307/270983>); for
    separated data, an exact linear-programming check for separation,
    likelihood-ratio tests from the suprema of the log-likelihood (Rainey 2024
    <doi:10.1017/pan.2023.28>), and penalized estimates of the multinomial,
    conditional, and rank-ordered logit under Firth's (1993)
    <doi:10.1093/biomet/80.1.27> penalty or weakly informative priors
    (Gelman, Jakulin, Pittau and Su 2008 <doi:10.1214/08-AOAS191>); predicted
    probabilities, first differences, and relative risk ratios; fit statistics,
    Vuong (1989) <doi:10.2307/1912557>, likelihood-ratio, and
    parametric-bootstrap comparisons; the existing
    battery of tests of the independence of irrelevant alternatives, Hausman and
    McFadden (1984) <doi:10.2307/1910997>, Small and Hsiao (1985)
    <doi:10.2307/2526707>, the composite-likelihood Hausman and GMM
    overidentification tests of Fok and Paap (2025)
    <doi:10.1016/j.jocm.2024.100531>, and the nested-logit score test of
    McFadden (1987) <doi:10.1016/0304-4076(87)90067-4>; score and
    likelihood-ratio tests for captive choosers against the dogit (Tse 1987
    <doi:10.1080/07350015.1987.10509587>; Self and Liang 1987
    <doi:10.1080/01621459.1987.10478472>); and the specification
    tests of Cramer and Ridder (1991) <doi:10.1016/0304-4076(91)90102-J> and
    Fagerland, Hosmer and Bofin (2008) <doi:10.1002/sim.3202>. The likelihoods
    and their gradients are implemented in 'C++'.
